  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":1,"t":"single_choice","p":"An arithmetic sequence begins 7, 12, 17, 22, … What is the 20th term?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An arithmetic sequence begins 7, 12, 17, 22, … What is the 20th term?"}]}]},"e":"The common difference is 5 and u_n = 7 + 5(n − 1), so u_20 = 7 + 5(19) = 102. Adding 5 twenty times (107) counts one step too many.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"nth term of an arithmetic sequence","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":1,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"u_n = u_1 + (n − 1)d: the first term is already there, so there are only n − 1 steps.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["102","107","100","97"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":2,"t":"single_choice","p":"For the sequence 4, 9, 14, 19, … what is the sum of the first 15 terms?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the sequence 4, 9, 14, 19, … what is the sum of the first 15 terms?"}]}]},"e":"d = 5, so the 15th term is 4 + 14(5) = 74. Then S_15 = 15/2 × (4 + 74) = 7.5 × 78 = 585.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic series","st":"sum of an arithmetic series","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"S_n = n/2 (u_1 + u_n). Find the last term first; forgetting the final term is the usual error.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["585","555","600","1 170"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"A geometric sequence is 3, 6, 12, 24, … What is its 8th term?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence is 3, 6, 12, 24, … What is its 8th term?"}]}]},"e":"r = 2, so u_8 = 3 × 2^7 = 3 × 128 = 384. Using 2^8 would add one extra doubling.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"nth term of a geometric sequence","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"u_n = u_1 r^(n−1). Check the index by counting: the 2nd term uses r^1.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["384","768","192","48"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence 80, 40, 20, 10, …?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence 80, 40, 20, 10, …?"}]}]},"e":"Each term is half the previous one: 40 ÷ 80 = 0.5, and 20 ÷ 40 = 0.5 confirms it. Subtracting (40) would be the arithmetic difference, not the ratio.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"identifying the common ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A common ratio is found by dividing a term by the one before it, not by subtracting.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["0.5","40, because the terms decrease by 40","2","−0.5"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"A sequence is defined by u_1 = 4 and u_{n+1} = 3u_n − 2. What is u_3?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sequence is defined by u_1 = 4 and u_{n+1} = 3u_n − 2. What is u_3?"}]}]},"e":"u_2 = 3(4) − 2 = 10, then u_3 = 3(10) − 2 = 28. Applying the rule twice to u_1 (giving u_2 twice) is the most common slip.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"recurrence relations","st":"iterating a recurrence relation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Work term by term: each application of the rule produces exactly one new term.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["28","10","82","34"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the value of Σ (from k = 1 to 5) of (2k + 1)?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the value of Σ (from k = 1 to 5) of (2k + 1)?"}]}]},"e":"The terms are 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, which total 35. Substituting only the upper limit (2(5) + 1 = 11) gives a single term, not the sum.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"sigma notation","st":"evaluating sigma notation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Sigma means ''add the terms'': list them or use the arithmetic-sum formula.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["35","11","30","36"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the sum to infinity of 12 + 6 + 3 + 1.5 + …?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the sum to infinity of 12 + 6 + 3 + 1.5 + …?"}]}]},"e":"r = 0.5 and |r| < 1, so S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 12/0.5 = 24. The sum is finite even though infinitely many terms are added.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"sum to infinity","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"S∞ = a/(1 − r) only when |r| < 1; check that condition before using the formula.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["24","22.5","It does not exist because the series is infinite","48"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which infinite geometric series converges to a finite sum?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which infinite geometric series converges to a finite sum?"}]}]},"e":"A geometric series converges exactly when |r| < 1. Of the four options only the one with r = 1/3 satisfies that; r = 2, r = 1.5 and r = 1 all diverge.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"convergence condition","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Convergence needs |r| < 1. Test the ratio of consecutive terms before summing infinitely.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["5 + 5/3 + 5/9 + 5/27 + …","1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + …","4 + 6 + 9 + 13.5 + …","7 + 7 + 7 + 7 + …"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"$2 000 is invested at 5% per year compound interest. What is its value after 3 years?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$2 000 is invested at 5% per year compound interest. What is its value after 3 years?"}]}]},"e":"2000 × 1.05³ = 2000 × 1.157625 = $2 315.25. Adding 3 × 5% = 15% of $2 000 gives $2 300, which ignores that interest is earned on previous interest.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"compound interest","st":"compound interest","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compound growth multiplies repeatedly: A = P(1 + r)^n. Simple interest adds only P × r × n.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["$2 315.25","$2 300.00","$2 315.00","$3 000.00"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":10,"t":"single_choice","p":"A machine costing $8 000 depreciates by 12% of its value each year. What is its value after 2 years?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A machine costing "},{"type":"text","text":"$8 000 depreciates by 12% of its value each year. What is its value after 2 years?"}]}]},"e":"8000 × 0.88² = 8000 × 0.7744 = $6 195.20. Subtracting 24% of $8 000 ($6 080) is wrong because the second year''s 12% is taken from a smaller value.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"depreciation","st":"depreciation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Depreciation is repeated multiplication by (1 − rate), never repeated subtraction of the same amount.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["$6 195.20","$6 080.00","$7 040.00","$6 272.00"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":11,"t":"single_choice","p":"Solve the exponential equation 3^x = 81 and state the value of x.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solve the exponential equation 3^x = 81 and state the value of x."}]}]},"e":"81 = 3^4, so x = 4. Dividing 81 by 3 gives 27, which is the value of 3^x for x = 3, not the exponent itself.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"exponential equations","st":"solving by matching bases","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":11,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Write both sides as powers of the same base, then equate the exponents.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 4","x = 27","x = 3","x = 40.5"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":12,"t":"single_choice","p":"Without a calculator, state the value of log base 2 of 32.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Without a calculator, state the value of log base 2 of 32."}]}]},"e":"log₂ 32 asks ''2 raised to what power gives 32?'' Since 32 = 2^5, the answer is 5.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"logarithms","st":"evaluating a logarithm","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A logarithm is an exponent: log_b x = y means b^y = x.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["5","16","2.5","6"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":13,"t":"single_choice","p":"By writing both sides as powers of 2, solve 4^x = 64 and state the value of x.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By writing both sides as powers of 2, solve 4^x = 64 and state the value of x."}]}]},"e":"4 = 2² and 64 = 2^6, so 2^(2x) = 2^6, giving 2x = 6 and x = 3. Dividing 64 by 4 (giving 16) is the value of 4², not the solution.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"exponential equations","st":"solving by matching bases","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Convert both sides to the same base: 4^x = (2²)^x = 2^(2x).","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 3","x = 16","x = 1.5","x = 2"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":14,"t":"single_choice","p":"By factorising, state the solutions of the quadratic equation x² − 5x + 6 = 0.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By factorising, state the solutions of the quadratic equation x² − 5x + 6 = 0."}]}]},"e":"Factorising gives (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3. The pair −2 and −3 multiplies to +6 but sums to −5 only if both signs are negative — which they are not here.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"solving a quadratic by factorising","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"For x² + bx + c find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 2 and x = 3","x = −2 and x = −3","x = 1 and x = 6","x = 5 and x = 6"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"The curve y = 2(x + 1)² + 7 is a parabola. What are the coordinates of its turning point?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The curve y = 2(x + 1)² + 7 is a parabola. What are the coordinates of its turning point?"}]}]},"e":"In completed-square form y = a(x − h)² + k the turning point is (h, k). Here h = −1 (because x + 1 = x − (−1)) and k = 7, so the minimum is at (−1, 7).","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"turning point from completed square","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"y = a(x − h)² + k has its turning point at (h, k); watch the sign inside the bracket.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["(−1, 7)","(1, 7)","(−1, −7)","(2, 7)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"By evaluating the discriminant of the equation x² + 4x + 9 = 0, what can you conclude about its roots?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By evaluating the discriminant of the equation x² + 4x + 9 = 0, what can you conclude about its roots?"}]}]},"e":"b² − 4ac = 16 − 36 = −20. A negative discriminant means the equation has no real roots, so the curve does not meet the x-axis.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"the discriminant","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Δ = b² − 4ac: negative means no real roots, zero means a repeated root, positive means two roots.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["It is negative, so there are no real roots","It is negative, so there are two equal real roots","It is positive, so there are two distinct real roots","It is zero, so the curve touches the x-axis"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"Simplify (2^5 × 2^−3) ÷ 2^4 and state the value of the result.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Simplify (2^5 × 2^−3) ÷ 2^4 and state the value of the result."}]}]},"e":"2^5 × 2^−3 = 2^2, and 2^2 ÷ 2^4 = 2^−2 = 1/4. Adding all three exponents (giving 2^6) treats a division as a multiplication.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"indices","st":"laws of indices","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Multiplying adds exponents, dividing subtracts them, and a negative exponent means a reciprocal.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["1/4","4","64","1/2"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"Solve 5^(2x − 1) = 125 and state the value of x.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solve 5^(2x − 1) = 125 and state the value of x."}]}]},"e":"125 = 5³, so 2x − 1 = 3 and x = 2. Setting 2x − 1 = 125 divides instead of matching exponents.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"exponential equations","st":"solving by matching bases","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Rewrite 125 as a power of 5 first, then equate the exponents and solve the linear equation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 2","x = 63","x = 1","x = 2.5"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"A geometric sequence has u_1 = 5 and r = −2. What is u_6?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence has u_1 = 5 and r = −2. What is u_6?"}]}]},"e":"u_6 = 5 × (−2)^5 = 5 × (−32) = −160. The sign alternates, so an even-indexed term is now negative: u_6 uses an odd power of −2.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"negative common ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A negative ratio alternates signs; (−2)^5 is negative because the power is odd.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["−160","160","−320","−62"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"An infinite geometric series has first term a and common ratio r. Which condition guarantees a finite sum?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An infinite geometric series has first term a and common ratio r. Which condition guarantees a finite sum?"}]}]},"e":"The formula S∞ = a/(1 − r) requires |r| < 1, which is what makes the terms shrink towards zero fast enough for the total to settle.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"limits of series","st":"convergence condition","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Only a ratio smaller than 1 in size makes the successive terms shrink.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["|r| < 1","r > 0","a < r","|r| > 1"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"Three numbers form an arithmetic sequence. The first term is 5 and the third term is 21. What is the middle number?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three numbers form an arithmetic sequence. The first term is 5 and the third term is 21. What is the middle number?"}]}]},"e":"In an arithmetic sequence the middle term is the mean of its neighbours: (5 + 21)/2 = 13, giving d = 8. Halving the difference (8) alone gives the step, not the middle term.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"arithmetic mean","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"With equal steps, the middle term is exactly halfway between the outer two.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["13","8","16","26"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two membership models both start at $100. Model L adds $3 for every visit. Model E multiplies the total by 1.5 for every block of visits completed, so it grows by a constant factor. Which statement is justified?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two membership models both start at "},{"type":"text","text":"$100. Model L adds $"},{"type":"text","text":"3 for every visit. Model E multiplies the total by 1.5 for every block of visits completed, so it grows by a constant factor. Which statement is justified?"}]}]},"e":"A linear model (100 + 3n) grows by a constant amount each visit, while a doubling model grows by a constant factor. The exponential model starts lower but inevitably overtakes the linear one and then grows far faster, so ''linear is always cheaper'' is false.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"comparing linear and exponential growth","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Linear growth adds a constant; exponential growth multiplies by a constant, so it eventually dominates.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["The doubling model eventually overtakes the linear one and then grows far faster","The linear model is always cheaper because it starts lower","Both models grow at the same rate because both start at 100","The doubling model exceeds the linear one after every single visit"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""}]'::jsonb) as x
on conflict (id) do nothing;

